<Base
  title="About - Nepal Market Data"
  description="What this dataset contains, how it is collected, and its known quirks."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About this dataset</h1>

    <p>
      Nepal Market Data is a free, open, daily-updated historical dataset covering three kinds of
      Nepali financial instruments: NEPSE-listed stocks (plus the NEPSE index itself), precious
      metals (gold and silver rates), and SIP mutual fund net asset values. Every row is stored as
      plain CSV, one file per symbol, and the raw files are the same ones this site reads at
      runtime: nothing here is a derived or summarized copy.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in each dataset</h2>
    <p>
      The NEPSE stock and index files, under <code>data/nepse/</code>, carry a published date,
      open, high, low, and close price, and traded volume where the source reports it (the index
      itself has no volume). The precious metals files, under <code>data/precious-metals/</code>,
      carry a published date and a price per unit. The mutual fund files, under
      <code>data/sip-mutual-funds/</code>, carry a published date and a net asset value, or NAV.
    </p>

    <h2>Rows are source-dated, not run-dated</h2>
    <p>
      Every row's date is the date its source says the value applies to, not the date the scraper
      happened to run. A scraper that runs late, or catches up after an outage, still writes the
      row under the date the source published it for, not the date it was fetched.
    </p>

    <h2>NAV data is a day behind by design</h2>
    <p>
      Mutual fund NAVs are inherently at least one day behind the calendar. Every source in this
      dataset posts day D's NAV on day D+1, after the fund's own end-of-day valuation is complete.
      A missing NAV for today is expected, not an error: today's NAV will not exist anywhere yet,
      including on the source's own site.
    </p>

    <h2>Weekday-only publishers</h2>
    <p>
      Two fund sources, <code>CSBY</code> and <code>SFF</code>, publish NAVs on weekdays only. Gaps
      in their files over Saturdays and Sundays are expected and are not a scraper failure.
    </p>

    <h2>Gaps are real, nothing is fabricated</h2>
    <p>
      More generally, weekend and public holiday gaps across every dataset are real reflections of
      days the underlying market or source did not publish a value. No row is ever fabricated,
      interpolated, or carried forward to fill a gap. If a date is missing from a file, it is
      because the source had nothing to report for that date.
    </p>

    <h2>Sector is the source's own vocabulary</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>sector</code> field on each stock reference is copied as published by the source,
      not mapped onto a standard industry taxonomy. Values vary in specificity and naming from what
      you might expect. One entry is a known misnomer: <code>government_bond</code> is the source's
      label for a sector that in fact contains only bank bonds, not government bonds. It is kept
      verbatim rather than silently corrected, so that anyone comparing this dataset against the
      original source sees the same value in both places.
    </p>

